# What must never reach a built image.
#
# There was no .dockerignore at all, and the Dockerfile ends with `COPY . .`, so
# the build context was whatever happened to be in the working tree. Two
# consequences, both real:
#
#   * `.env` was copied into a layer. It holds SECRET_KEY, JWT_SECRET_KEY,
#     FERNET_KEY and the database credentials. Anyone who can pull the image can
#     read them, and deleting the file in a later layer does not remove it from
#     the earlier one. Configuration is injected at run time; it is never built in.
#
#   * Plugin directories are gitignored but NOT dockerignored, so a build run
#     from a developer's tree silently included plugins that a clean checkout
#     does not have - which is how a build could fail on a dependency nobody
#     had deliberately installed.
#
# Plugins are still installable: the Dockerfile copies the plugin trees
# explicitly, so putting a directory there and rebuilding works exactly as the
# install documentation describes. What this file stops is the *accidental*
# inclusion of everything else.

# ---- secrets and local configuration ----
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key

# ---- version control and editor state ----
.git
.gitignore
.github
.vscode
.idea

# ---- runtime data mounts, not code ----
/original
/webfiles
/userfiles
/temporal
/uploads

# ---- build and test artefacts ----
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
*.egg-info
htmlcov
.coverage

# ---- not needed to run the service ----
tests
tools
node_modules
requirements.txt.bak

# ---- documentation and notes ----
*.md
!README.md
